  "query_preview": "(Today through Sunday)...\nIssued at 307 AM EST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nToday through Tuesday night...\n\nQuiet weather conditions are expected to persist as upper ridging \nand surface high pressure build eastward into the region. Current \nsatellite and surface observations depict surface high pressure \ncentered near the central CONUS with clear skies across Indiana. A \nvery weak pressure gradient is contributing to light or even calm \nwinds at times early this morning. These light winds combined with \nclear skies could allow for patchy fog to develop in \nclimatologically favored locations towards daybreak. Any fog that \ndevelops should quickly mix out after sunrise.   \n\nModel guidance depicts a shortwave moving across the Great Lakes \nRegion around Tuesday morning with an associated weak cold front \npushing into central Indiana. No precipitation is expected from the \napproaching boundary due to gulf moisture remaining cutoff. The weak \nfrontal boundary could cool temperatures slightly. \n\nReturn flow will moderate temperatures through Tuesday before the \ncold front arrives. Look for highs to generally range from the low \nto mid 60s with lows in the upper 30s to low 40s. Highs on Tuesday \ncould end up being a few degrees cooler than currently forecasted \ndepending on when the front arrives. A quicker FROPA Tuesday morning \nwould likely limit daytime heating. \n\nWednesday through early next week... \n\nA deepening shortwave trough moving in from the northwest on \nWednesday is expected to promote surface cyclogenesis across the \nMidwest. Latest ensemble guidance depicts the surface low developing \nnear Illinois and continuing to strengthen as it progresses \neastward. Increasing dynamics from these features combined with \nsufficient gulf moisture surging northward supports increasing rain \nchances. Scattered to numerous showers are possible and weak \ndestabilization late Wednesday may support a few thunderstorms. \n\nThe parent trough is expected to continue deepening across the \neastern CONUS through late this week with strong cold air advection \nacross central Indiana. Broad cyclonic flow, steepening lapse rates, \nand lingering low-level moisture will continue to promote the \npotential for showers on Thursday. Latest guidance continues to \nsuggest precipitation will linger through Friday given a favorable \nfetch off of Lake Michigan. Isolated rain/snow showers are possible \nto end the work week, but QPF amounts are expected to be minimal due \nto very limited moisture and weak forcing.\n\nThe aforementioned low pressure system and parent trough will shift \neastward over the weekend while an amplified upper ridge builds into \nthe central CONUS. Warm air advection ahead of the upper ridge is \nexpected to help moderate temperatures over the weekend into early \nnext week. Drier conditions are also likely. Look for a \nroller coaster in temperatures from the middle of this week \nthrough early next week. Highs in the low to mid 60s Wednesday \nwill quickly cool down well into the 40s by Friday before then \nwarming back up into the 50s on Sunday.",
